// A `const` is compile time; a `let` is not.
//
// `use` is evaluated while the program is compiled, so its body may only reach
// names that already have compile-time values. `helper` is bound by `let`, so
// it has no value until the program runs — there is nothing for the
// specialized definition to close over, and a definition hoisted to the top
// level has no enclosing frame to find it in.
//
// The refusal points at the capture rather than at `helper`'s declaration:
// `helper` is perfectly in scope here, it is in the wrong phase. Writing
// `let use = fn y => helper y;` compiles.

open import "blot:prelude"

let helper = fn x => x + 1
const use = fn y => helper y

export use 1
